Great instinct — the "N1 / I1" bubble approach is dated and doesn't communicate much. Let me suggest something better before I write it.

My recommendation: A living landscape canvas with layered visual metaphors

Instead of abstract badges, render an actual top-down landscape grid — a 20×12 "land parcel" — where each cell is a small plot of land. Each cell's appearance is driven by three variables simultaneously:

1. Colour (soil health gradient) — deep forest green → olive → dusty tan → cracked grey. This is the heatmap layer, but grounded in something recognisable (healthy land vs. degraded land).
2. Species glyphs — instead of "N1/I1" text, small SVG icons: a native shrub/grass tuft (green) and an invasive gorse spike (red/orange), scattered with density proportional to native vs. invasive coverage. Cells with high invasives get red-tinted soil, cells with high natives get green tint.
3. Pulse / shimmer — healthy cells subtly animate (CSS keyframes) so the landscape feels alive; degraded cells are static and flat. This is the "2026" touch without being gratuitous.

Plus three live stat readouts below the canvas: Native Cover %, Invasive Cover %, and a Trajectory indicator (Declining / Stabilising / Recovering / Thriving) — because a single progress bar hides the ecology. Rainfall, effort, and invasive pressure each modulate specific visual dimensions, so the user sees each slider do something distinct:

· Rainfall → baseline soil colour saturation (drier = more tan/brown tones)
· Invasive pressure → how many invasive glyphs spawn and how aggressively they cluster
· Restoration effort → how many native glyphs spawn and how quickly cells recover colour
· Time → how far along the recovery gradient the whole canvas has shifted

Every Run re-generates the grid with a seeded random layout, so it feels alive but reproducible per run. I'll also add a legend and hover tooltips on cells (e.g. "Plot 4-7 · Native: high · Invasive: moderate").

